VERDICT: PASS
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: S1
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Attacked recurringOwnerAccount / Project in a scratch copy (/tmp/checker-scratch-budget2,
outside the repo; deleted after use). Repo itself untouched (git status clean except the
worker's own pending diff).

1. Tie-break order-independence. Wrote scratch tests calling recurringOwnerAccount directly
   with legs in forward and reversed order (2-way and 3-way permutations, exact-date ties).
   Owner was identical regardless of slice order in every permutation
   (TestScratch_OwnerOrderIndependence, TestScratch_TieBreakOrderIndependence,
   TestScratch_ThreeWayTie all PASS). The running-max reduction is not first-wins, so it is
   genuinely order-independent, matching the doc comment's claim.

2. Empty/missing data. No-legs RecurringPayment -> owner "" (TestScratch_NoLegs PASS). A
   series whose legs are ALL AccountID=="" (unassigned rows) -> owner "" too
   (TestScratch_AllLegsUnassigned).

3. Zero-value trap (the angle explicitly called out). Constructed an Account{ID: ""} and fed
   it an all-unassigned-legs series: recurringOwnerAccount(rp) == "" == acct.ID, so the
   filter `recurringOwnerAccount(rp) != acct.ID` is FALSE and the series IS applied in full
   to a hypothetical empty-ID account (Minimum dropped from 1000 to 1, confirming the
   collision). This is a real latent defect in the design. However I verified it is NOT
   reachable in production: internal/services/accounts/accounts.go:217 rejects
   "account ... has an empty ID" at save time (Validate), and both Project call sites
   (internal/handlers/dashboard/accounts_card.go:190, internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/accounts.go:241)
   only ever pass an Account looked up by findAccount, which returns ok=false (and the
   caller errors out) rather than a zero-value Account when no match exists. So no live
   caller can ever pass acct.ID == "". Noted as a design footgun, not a live bug -- not
   sufficient alone to fail the task, since the invariant it depends on is enforced
   elsewhere and documented as such nowhere is contradicted.

4. Regression, single-account case: TestProject_SingleAccountSeriesUnaffected (worker's own
   test) and TestScratch checks both confirm an unchanged result for a series confined to
   one account.

5. Verified the new tests are not vacuous: swapped projection.go for the pre-fix version
   (git show HEAD:...) in the scratch copy, kept the new test file, and reran. Both
   TestProject_RecurringAttributedToNewestLegOnly and
   TestProject_FormerAccountGetsNoCrossingOrTopUp go RED against the old code (cardA wrongly
   drained to 800, oldCard wrongly crosses at 400/500) -- these are real regression guards,
   not tautologies.

6. Scope creep: diffed projection.go hunk-by-hunk (git diff). Only four hunks touch: the two
   package/function doc comments, the filter call site (recurringBelongsToAccount ->
   recurringOwnerAccount), and the recurringOwnerAccount function body itself.
   calendarDayKey, utcCalendarDay, dayOf, frequencyIntervalDays, BalanceAt and the horizon
   walk are referenced only as unchanged context, never modified. `grep -rn` for
   recurringOwnerAccount/BelongsToAccount confirms no touch to internal/services/insights.
   go build ./... and go vet ./... are clean; go test ./... is green repo-wide (all "ok",
   web has no test files).

7. Instrumentation: no fmt.Println/log.Print/DEBUG in the diff or the resulting file.

8. Doc-vs-code drift -- REAL FINDING, does not rise to a FAIL but is worth recording: the
   pre-existing test TestProject_MixedRecurringFiltersByAccount (internal/services/accounts/
   projection_test.go, ~line 373-399) was left untouched by this change. Its comment still
   reads "It qualifies for usaa (recurringBelongsToAccount is true)" -- a function that no
   longer exists anywhere in the codebase (confirmed by repo-wide grep, only remaining hit is
   this comment). Worse: the test still passes under the NEW code, but only by coincidence --
   its two legs ("usaa", "schwab") share the same date, and the new tie-break happens to pick
   "usaa" (lexicographically greater) as the owner, which is what the test asserts. The test
   name and comment now describe semantics ("filters by account", "any leg" membership) the
   code no longer implements; it survives as an accidental pass, not a verified guarantee.
   REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md's S1 acceptance criteria only requires
   "existing test 'other accounts' recurring items are excluded' still passes unchanged" (a
   different test, TestProject_OtherAccountsRecurringExcluded, which is a true single-account
   exclusion case unaffected by tie-breaking) -- it says nothing about
   TestProject_MixedRecurringFiltersByAccount, so this is not a violation of a named
   acceptance criterion, but it is a stale/misleading test the worker should have either
   updated or removed given the mandated rule change directly invalidates its rationale.

9. Callers: both call sites (dashboard accounts_card.go, mcpsvc/ledger/accounts.go) call only
   accounts.Project(...); neither references recurringBelongsToAccount by name, so the rename
   has no collateral call-site breakage.

Net: could not construct an input that makes the mandated rule (most-recent-leg wins,
order-independent, single-account regression-safe, in-scope) misbehave. The one genuine
defect found (the acct.ID=="" / owner=="" collision) is unreachable given the existing
account-ID-non-empty invariant enforced at Validate() and at both call sites, so it does not
meet the bar for a reproducible production-input FAIL. The doc-drift in
TestProject_MixedRecurringFiltersByAccount is real and should be flagged back to the worker,
but it is a quality nit on an untouched pre-existing test, not a violation of the S1
acceptance criteria as written in REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md line 105.
